task:
Write a Ken program that:
1. determines the number of distinct customers among orders with product `coffee` and prints just the value;
2. classifies every one of the orders by its value `qty` × `price`: `small` if it is less than 100, `medium` if it is less than 900, and `large` otherwise. Print the number of orders in each class, alphabetically by class, in the format `class - count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. determines the average guest rating (`rating`) over all bookings and prints just the value (first print the text `Data:`);
2. computes the lowest nightly rate (`rate`) multiplied by 1.5 and rounded to 2 decimal places (define your own word `uplift` for the multiplication and rounding) for each guest, considering only bookings whose room type name contains the fragment `mi`, and then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `guest: value`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the total number of items (`qty`) for every product, then outputs the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `product: X pcs`;
2. computes the average number of items (`qty`) per order for each customer, considering only orders with category `clothing` or `groceries`, and then prints the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `customer: average` (first print the text `Summary:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each customer, the percentage of orders with status `done` among all orders of that customer, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the single entry with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `customer - X%`;
2. for each category determines the total revenue (`qty` × `price`), and then outputs the 3 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `category: X USD`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the shipments by its value `units` × `unit_cost`: `large` if it is at least 222, `medium` if it is at least 50, and `small` otherwise. Print the number of shipments in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`;
2. calculates the lowest unit cost (`unit_cost`) among shipments from the month `2026-02` and prints just the value (first print the text `Report:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each month, the percentage of bookings whose room type name contains the fragment `ngl` among all bookings of that month, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the results chronologically, one per line in the format `month: X%`;
2. computes the average guest rating (`rating`) for each room and labels each result: `good` if it is at least 3.7 and `poor` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by room type, in the format `room - label`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the lowest unit cost (`unit_cost`) among shipments from the month `2026-04` and prints just the value;
2. computes the lowest unit cost (`unit_cost`) among shipments whose item name contains the fragment `cel` and with client `falcon` or `granite` and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the number of distinct cities over all bookings and prints just the value (first print the text `Data:`);
2. calculates the ratio of the total booking value (`nights` × `rate`) to the number of distinct cities for every room type, considering only bookings where `nights` is greater than 4, rounded to 2 decimal places, then prints the 5 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `room: ratio`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. works out the total number of items (`qty`) for each product, and then prints the 2 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `product: pcs` (first print the text `Results:`);
2. computes the number of distinct customers for each product category, and then outputs all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `category - distinct`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the total weight in kilograms (`weight`) among shipments with item `tank` and prints just the value;
2. determines the average weight in kilograms (`weight`) for every result, considering only shipments where `weight` is less than 10, then prints the results alphabetically, one per line in the format `result: average`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. determines the number of distinct cities among bookings with room type `twin` and prints just the value (first print the text `Result:`);
2. for each month works out the highest guest rating (`rating`), and then displays the 5 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month: maximum`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. for each guest computes the highest guest rating (`rating`), considering only bookings where `rate` is at most 200, and then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `guest - maximum` (first print the text `Summary:`);
2. computes the total number of nights (`nights`) for each city and labels each result: `high` if it is at least 247 and `low` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by city, in the format `city - label`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the shipments by its `weight`: `bottom` if it is less than 19, `middle` if it is less than 106, and `top` otherwise, using your own word `bracket` for the rule. Print the number of shipments in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class - count`;
2. calculates the ratio of the total cost (`units` × `unit_cost`) to the number of shipments for each result, considering only shipments whose item name contains the fragment `tan`, rounded to 2 decimal places, and then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `result: ratio`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the average order value (`qty` × `price`) for every month, then prints the results chronologically, one per line in the format `month: average`;
2. computes the total revenue (`qty` × `price`) for each customer, considering only orders from the month `2026-03` and labels each result: `gold` if it is at least 17590, `silver` if it is at least 3810, and `bronze` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by customer, in the format `customer: label`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the number of distinct clients for each month, considering only shipments with region `east` or `south`, and then prints the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month; distinct` (first print the text `Results:`);
2. computes, for each region, the percentage of shipments with result `lost` among all shipments of that region, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then outputs alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 7.1, in the format `region: X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each room type, the percentage of bookings with outcome `cancelled` among all bookings of that room type, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `room: X%`;
2. works out the total number of nights (`nights`) among bookings where `rate` is at most 200 and with outcome `cancelled` and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the number of orders for each month, considering only orders with category `books` and where `qty` is at most 4, and then displays the 3 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `month: count` (first print the text `Overview:`);
2. computes, for each month, the percentage of orders with status other than `cancelled` among all orders of that month, considering only orders with category `electronics`, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then outputs the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month: X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the bookings by its value `nights` × `rate`: `good` if it is at least 390 and `poor` otherwise. Print the number of bookings in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`;
2. computes, for each month, the percentage of bookings with outcome `cancelled` among all bookings of that month, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints chronologically only the entries whose value is at least 19, in the format `month - X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the orders where `price` is greater than 300 by its value `qty` × `price`: `top` if it is at least 10500, `middle` if it is at least 1600, and `bottom` otherwise. Print the number of orders in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`;
2. computes the number of distinct categories for each month, considering only orders with product `comic` and where `price` is less than 250, and then outputs the 5 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month: distinct`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the highest unit cost (`unit_cost`) multiplied by 1.1 and rounded to 2 decimal places for every client, considering only shipments with region `south`, then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `client: value`;
2. calculates the average weight in kilograms (`weight`) for each client, and then displays the 2 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `client: X kg` (first print the text `Data:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the number of distinct categories over all orders and prints just the value;
2. computes, for each month, its share of the total number of items (`qty`) among orders with category `electronics`, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 2 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month: X%`.
